{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794528716484,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc4377affeMLBMP15YBCIej5","part":{"id":"prt_1d23f26c4001RLv5Hw7NJqwt5V","sessionID":"ses_e2dc4377affeMLBMP15YBCIej5","messageID":"msg_1d23f20ff0017E6WXHsZxla7q3","type":"step-start","snapshot":"6e89878d50496e6be9798a65a844b30bfb12aa16"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794528717934,"sessionID":"ses_e2dc4377affeMLBMP15YBCIej5","part":{"partID":"prt_1d23f2944001sN9BrIyjSbK2Zt","sessionID":"ses_e2dc4377affeMLBMP15YBCIej5","messageID":"msg_1d23f20ff0017E6WXHsZxla7q3","type":"tool","id":"call_b645f99f438c490b9be5bc6e","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n 1,80p .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-implement.md; sed -n 1,60p .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-verify.md","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"---\ntitle: Workflow Reference - Implementation\ndescription: Shared sk-code doctrine for the mutating build phase, from research before a change to the handoff into quality and verification.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code implementation workflow\"\n  - \"implement workflow doctrine\"\n  - \"mutating build workflow\"\n  - \"surface implementation standards\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: general\nversion: 2.1.6.0\n---\n\n# Workflow Reference - Implementation\n\nOpen this when you are about to change behavior and need to know how much to read before the first edit.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nImplementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n- Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n- Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n- Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n- Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n\n### When Not to Use\n\n- A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n- The code is written and needs author-side P0/P1/P2 checks, comment hygiene or surface checklists. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode, which runs `quality-gate.cjs` first.\n- The task is final non-mutating evidence. Use [verification](./workflow-verify.md).\n- The user wants findings-first review output instead of workspace edits. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n- The change is documentation-only prose with no executable behavior or routing change.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRead first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WORKFLOW\n\n### Research\n\nResearch comes before any change to unfamiliar or high-blast code. For a small local edit it can be brief, but it always includes reading the target file.\n\n1. Read every target file in this session before editing it.\n2. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports). Read them, with nearby conventions and existing examples, before introducing a new shape.\n3. Use the surface the shared router resolved ([stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md)). Never re-detect or override it inside this phase.\n4. Load the smallest active-surface resource set that covers the intent and the changed file types.\n5. For broad or risky work, run a bounded read-only research sweep before editing.\n6. When design or other upstream guidance handed off locked values, constraints or risks, keep them as given instead of redesigning the task.\n\n### Pre-Write Restraint\n\nBefore the first edit of non-trivial work, record two facts:\n\n| Field | Content |\n| --- | --- |\n| Baseline | The starting state, in the shape of the Baseline row in the verification [Baseline And Delta](./workflow-verify.md#baseline-and-delta) table. Write `UNKNOWN` when no safe baseline exists, because verification then has to settle for a narrower claim. |\n| Blast Radius | One phrase, such as `low-blast, reversible`, `medium-blast: multi-file behavior` or `high-blast: touches auth/data/filesystem/config`. |\n\nThen walk the [Design Restraint Ladder](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md#design-restraint-ladder-pre-write) and stop at the first rung that holds. The ladder never cuts a P0 item or anything the user asked for. When requested scope looks unnecessary or risky, implement the requirement and raise a scope-amendment recommendation in the same response. Never cut scope silently. The restraint floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md` sections 1 and 2 and the Restraint Signals table in `AGENTS.md` section 3.\n\n### Write\n\n1. Write the smallest correct change that meets the request and the active-surface standards.\n2. Keep the project's existing conventions unless the request changes them.\n3. Stay inside the user's scope and the established documentation scope.\n4. Keep comments durable and explanatory. Never add artifact labels or temporary process markers, because they go stale the moment the work that named them closes.\n5. Keep routing metadata, config shapes, generated-metadata ownership and packet boundaries intact when authoring system assets.\n6. Prepare the handoff: changed scope, baseline, likely checks and accepted residual risks.\n\n---\ntitle: Workflow Reference - Verification\ndescription: Shared sk-code doctrine for the final non-mutating evidence phase that gates every done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code verification workflow\"\n  - \"verify workflow doctrine\"\n  - \"falsifier discipline\"\n  - \"completion claim gate\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: general\nversion: 2.2.7.0\n---\n\n# Workflow Reference - Verification\n\nOpen this before you claim a change is finished, and when you need a falsifier that could disprove it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nVerification owns evidence, not fixes. It runs fresh surface-appropriate commands, records the outcomes, compares them against the implementation baseline, names the blind spots and claims only what the evidence proves. When verification fails, it hands back to debugging, implementation or quality without editing.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Verifying work after implementation, quality checks or debugging.\n- Running final commands before a done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n- Collecting evidence for active-surface behavior or system-code changes.\n- Reporting the delta from the baseline captured before implementation.\n- Running a mutation check or claim falsifier after a green result.\n- Walking the verification ladder and naming each rung's blind spot.\n\n### When Not to Use\n\n- The task needs code written, files scaffolded or behavior changed. Use [implementation](./workflow-implement.md).\n- The task is author-side quality cleanup. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode.\n- Verification finds a defect that needs root-cause repair. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n- The user wants findings-first review output or PR-style critique. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n- The active surface and verification command set are unknown. Ask for them before claiming anything.\n\n### Iron Law\n\n**Iron Law**: no completion claim without fresh surface-appropriate verification evidence.\n\nA clean static read is not runtime evidence. A green unit test is not live proof. A successful build is not exercised behavior. One exercised path is not full regression coverage. Name the rung you reached and its blind spot.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WORKFLOW\n\n### Verification Loop\n\n1. Resolve the active surface through [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md). If it is unsupported or ambiguous, ask for the verification command set before claiming anything. The quality config, `repository/quality.json` in the `sk-code-quality` packet, names quality checks only, so it never supplies the verification command set.\n2. Read the implementation, quality or debug handoff for the intended behavior, changed scope, baseline and accepted risks.\n   - From the quality envelope, read the `checker_outputs` field with the `gate` and `unverified` values it carries, and any field that still reads `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`.\n   - Hand back to quality only on `BLOCKED`, `ERROR` or a field still reading `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`, which is the 3 to 1.5 transition in [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n   - On `UNVERIFIED`, prove each id it lists with this phase's own command or report that id open.\n   - On `NOTHING_CHECKED`, prove the claim with this phase's own commands and never read the gate as clean, because no check read any file as code, which is where a Markdown-only change lands.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\ntitle: Workflow Reference - Implementation\ndescription: Shared sk-code doctrine for the mutating build phase, from research before a change to the handoff into quality and verification.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code implementation workflow\"\n  - \"implement workflow doctrine\"\n  - \"mutating build workflow\"\n  - \"surface implementation standards\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: general\nversion: 2.1.6.0\n---\n\n# Workflow Reference - Implementation\n\nOpen this when you are about to change behavior and need to know how much to read before the first edit.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nImplementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n- Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n- Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n- Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n- Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n\n### When Not to Use\n\n- A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n- The code is written and needs author-side P0/P1/P2 checks, comment hygiene or surface checklists. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode, which runs `quality-gate.cjs` first.\n- The task is final non-mutating evidence. Use [verification](./workflow-verify.md).\n- The user wants findings-first review output instead of workspace edits. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n- The change is documentation-only prose with no executable behavior or routing change.\n\n### Core Principle\n\nRead first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WORKFLOW\n\n### Research\n\nResearch comes before any change to unfamiliar or high-blast code. For a small local edit it can be brief, but it always includes reading the target file.\n\n1. Read every target file in this session before editing it.\n2. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports). Read them, with nearby conventions and existing examples, before introducing a new shape.\n3. Use the surface the shared router resolved ([stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md)). Never re-detect or override it inside this phase.\n4. Load the smallest active-surface resource set that covers the intent and the changed file types.\n5. For broad or risky work, run a bounded read-only research sweep before editing.\n6. When design or other upstream guidance handed off locked values, constraints or risks, keep them as given instead of redesigning the task.\n\n### Pre-Write Restraint\n\nBefore the first edit of non-trivial work, record two facts:\n\n| Field | Content |\n| --- | --- |\n| Baseline | The starting state, in the shape of the Baseline row in the verification [Baseline And Delta](./workflow-verify.md#baseline-and-delta) table. Write `UNKNOWN` when no safe baseline exists, because verification then has to settle for a narrower claim. |\n| Blast Radius | One phrase, such as `low-blast, reversible`, `medium-blast: multi-file behavior` or `high-blast: touches auth/data/filesystem/config`. |\n\nThen walk the [Design Restraint Ladder](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md#design-restraint-ladder-pre-write) and stop at the first rung that holds. The ladder never cuts a P0 item or anything the user asked for. When requested scope looks unnecessary or risky, implement the requirement and raise a scope-amendment recommendation in the same response. Never cut scope silently. The restraint floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md` sections 1 and 2 and the Restraint Signals table in `AGENTS.md` section 3.\n\n### Write\n\n1. Write the smallest correct change that meets the request and the active-surface standards.\n2. Keep the project's existing conventions unless the request changes them.\n3. Stay inside the user's scope and the established documentation scope.\n4. Keep comments durable and explanatory. Never add artifact labels or temporary process markers, because they go stale the moment the work that named them closes.\n5. Keep routing metadata, config shapes, generated-metadata ownership and packet boundaries intact when authoring system assets.\n6. Prepare the handoff: changed scope, baseline, likely checks and accepted residual risks.\n\n---\ntitle: Workflow Reference - Verification\ndescription: Shared sk-code doctrine for the final non-mutating evidence phase that gates every done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"sk-code verification workflow\"\n  - \"verify workflow doctrine\"\n  - \"falsifier discipline\"\n  - \"completion claim gate\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: general\nversion: 2.2.7.0\n---\n\n# Workflow Reference - Verification\n\nOpen this before you claim a change is finished, and when you need a falsifier that could disprove it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nVerification owns evidence, not fixes. It runs fresh surface-appropriate commands, records the outcomes, compares them against the implementation baseline, names the blind spots and claims only what the evidence proves. When verification fails, it hands back to debugging, implementation or quality without editing.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Verifying work after implementation, quality checks or debugging.\n- Running final commands before a done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n- Collecting evidence for active-surface behavior or system-code changes.\n- Reporting the delta from the baseline captured before implementation.\n- Running a mutation check or claim falsifier after a green result.\n- Walking the verification ladder and naming each rung's blind spot.\n\n### When Not to Use\n\n- The task needs code written, files scaffolded or behavior changed. Use [implementation](./workflow-implement.md).\n- The task is author-side quality cleanup. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode.\n- Verification finds a defect that needs root-cause repair. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n- The user wants findings-first review output or PR-style critique. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n- The active surface and verification command set are unknown. Ask for them before claiming anything.\n\n### Iron Law\n\n**Iron Law**: no completion claim without fresh surface-appropriate verification evidence.\n\nA clean static read is not runtime evidence. A green unit test is not live proof. A successful build is not exercised behavior. One exercised path is not full regression coverage. Name the rung you reached and its blind spot.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WORKFLOW\n\n### Verification Loop\n\n1. Resolve the active surface through [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md). If it is unsupported or ambiguous, ask for the verification command set before claiming anything. The quality config, `repository/quality.json` in the `sk-code-quality` packet, names quality checks only, so it never supplies the verification command set.\n2. Read the implementation, quality or debug handoff for the intended behavior, changed scope, baseline and accepted risks.\n   - From the quality envelope, read the `checker_outputs` field with the `gate` and `unverified` values it carries, and any field that still reads `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`.\n   - Hand back to quality only on `BLOCKED`, `ERROR` or a field still reading `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`, which is the 3 to 1.5 transition in [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n   - On `UNVERIFIED`, prove each id it lists with this phase's own command or report that id open.\n   - On `NOTHING_CHECKED`, prove the claim with this phase's own commands and never read the gate as clean, because no check read any file as code, which is where a Markdown-only change lands.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"sed -n 1,80p .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-implement.md; sed -n 1,60p .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-verify.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794528717124,"end":1794528717934}}}}
